WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:01.101 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:02.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
is an Emmy-nominated
and Golden Globe-winning actor

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:05.238
you know from his work
on "House, M.D.," "Veep,"

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:05.905 size:88% position:44% line:92%
and "The Night Manager."

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:08.141 align:start size:94% position:6%
He stars in the miniseries
"Roadkill,"

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:11.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
which airs its fourth and fina
episode Sunday night

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:13.079 size:81% position:41% line:92%
on Masterpiece on PBS.

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:13.780 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:14.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Let me tell you,
there is a reason

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:17.717
why this country has never moved
into the 21st century,

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:19.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's called
the civil service.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.187 line:92%
-I am aware of your view.

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:22.022 size:84% position:42% line:92%
You've made that plain.

00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:23.723 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Any time any minister
comes into office

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:27.727 align:start size:94% position:6%
with any trace of passion
or a serious idea for change

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:30.897
there you are in the best
traditions of your calling

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:32.098 line:92%
ready to throw a wet blanket

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:35.135
over anything that might improve
people's lives.

00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:37.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
Restore the missing clause
to the bill.

00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:40.140 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
That's an order.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.274 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Is there anything else?

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:43.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Please welcome to the sho
Hugh Laurie!

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:44.778 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
How are you, Hugh?

00:00:44.778 --> 00:00:46.046 line:92%
-I'm very well, thank you, Seth.

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:49.716 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you. Ha, ha, ha.
Ha, ha. Thank you.

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:50.417 line:92%
-I'm so happy to see you.

00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:53.353 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is your first time
on this show.

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:55.121 line:92%
And we've crossed paths before

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:58.758 align:start size:94% position:6%
but, you know, it's a deligh
to lay eyes on you again.

00:00:58.758 --> 00:00:59.359 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Crossed paths?

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:02.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it was a little more
than that in my memory.

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:04.397 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
I feel --

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:07.067 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, it was a momentous event
in my life.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, you hosted --
You hosted SNL.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.071 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I did.
-You were hosting it

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.138 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
because of "House."

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
I knew you as --
from "Blackadder."

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:17.444 align:start size:94% position:6%
I knew you from
"A Bit of Fry &amp; Laurie."

00:01:17.444 --> 00:01:20.580
I knew you from my favorit
episode of "The Young Ones."

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.215 line:92%
I knew you as a comedy person.

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:25.285
I was surprised to hear you were
nervous about hosting SNL.

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:27.420
-I was -- That was
the single most terrifying thing

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.524
I have ever done, and I have
jumped out of an airplane.

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:35.228
And this was considerably worse,
considerably worse.

00:01:35.228 --> 00:01:36.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
Although when I jumped
out of an airplane,

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:38.231 align:start size:94% position:6%
I had an instructor
tied to my back.

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:42.869
And in the case of SNL, you were
that instructor tied to my bac

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:44.838 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you guided me...
-[ Laughs ] That's so --

00:01:44.838 --> 00:01:47.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
-...with a gentle
and loving hand,

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.842 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I will forever
be in your debt.

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:51.711
I'm not going to repay the debt,
but I am --

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:53.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you for that.
-It's just nice

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:54.380 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you acknowledge
there is a debt.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.883 align:start size:94% position:6%
Whether it's repaid or not
it's just nice to know.

00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:58.318 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, that's the importan
thing.

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:00.820 align:start size:94% position:6%
-This show, I want to say
about "Roadkill,"

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.889 line:92%
this is exactly what I wanted.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:04.290 line:92%
It is my favorite kind of show

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:09.796 align:start size:94% position:6%
It is a PBS Masterpiece
British political intrigue

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:13.533
It's a limited miniseries.
It ticks every box for me.

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:17.437
How did you get involved,
and what drew you to it?

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:19.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I was drawn to it
by the name on the title page.

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:22.675
It was written by David Hare
who is a very respected --

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:26.146
I would say even revered
playwright in this country

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:28.248 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and in yours, too.

00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:29.716 line:92%
So that piqued my interest

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:32.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I thought
it was an interesting notion

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:36.723 align:start size:94% position:6%
to examine the whole idea
of political shame,

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:39.192
the fact that shame and disgrace
in the public sphere,

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:43.229 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the political sphere,
has just, sort of, vanished.

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:44.898 align:start size:94% position:6%
Weirdly, it's been
sort of replaced

00:02:44.898 --> 00:02:46.799 line:92%
by shame in the private sphere

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:49.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
or the sphere
of the private citizen.

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:54.240
But politicians now have
a completely different approach,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.975 align:start size:94% position:6%
completely different
relationship

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:57.744 size:88% position:44% line:92%
with their constituency.

00:02:57.744 --> 00:02:59.846 size:88% position:44%
And I thought that was
particularly interesting

00:02:59.846 --> 00:03:03.183 align:start size:94% position:6%
to examine it from the sort of
right-wing perspective.

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:04.918 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's been so much written
about the left.

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:08.588 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the right wing
generally goes un-remarked on.

00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:11.424 size:88% position:44%
We just assume they make
the trains run on time

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:12.192 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and nothing else.

00:03:12.192 --> 00:03:13.726 line:92%
But of course, they are capabl

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:15.528 align:start size:94% position:6%
of being every bit
as ideological,

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:17.263 line:92%
if not more so, than the left.

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:20.900
-It is fun to watch a show
that is both about politic

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.669 align:start size:94% position:6%
and about bad behavior
in politics

00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:25.939
that isn't tied directly t
the moment we're living through.

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:29.008
I mean, a lot of it overlaps
and you can certainly see,

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:30.410 align:start size:94% position:6%
certainly when we
talk about shame

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.945 line:92%
and being able to escape..

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:32.779 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right.
-...being shamed.

00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:37.050
But it must be fun to play
a sort of charismatic politician

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.119 size:88% position:44%
who is ultimately trying
to get away with stuff.

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:45.491
-Well, if it says charismati
in the character description

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:47.560 align:start size:94% position:6%
where are they gonna go,
you know?

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:50.330 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
I mean, how many --

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:50.897 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Yeah, it was.

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:52.966 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, as you say,
it was very peculiar.

00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:55.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
It seems peculiar to me no
looking back.

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:59.906 align:start size:94% position:6%
We started it just over --
about a year ago.

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:02.575 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think there's
just one mention of Brexit

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:03.476 line:92%
no mention of the pandemic

00:04:03.476 --> 00:04:05.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
even though it was kind of
on the horizon.

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:09.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it seems
so distant now to me.

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:10.316 size:84% position:42% line:92%
It seems almost quaint,

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:13.119 align:start size:94% position:6%
like we're doing
something about Tudor England.

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:15.888 line:92%
It's -- The world has change

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:18.558 align:start size:94% position:6%
out of all recognition
in 12 months.

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:25.265
And I would like to offer it
as a -- as a parallel universe

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:26.399 line:92%
where none of the characters

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:28.101 align:start size:94% position:6%
are drawn from
contemporary politics.

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:31.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's a story
set in a parallel universe

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:34.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I'm a parallel person,
which, actually --

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:36.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think we need
more parallel universes.

00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:40.346 align:start size:94% position:6%
We need to take just a light
break from this universe.

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:41.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's exactly right.
-But it is funny

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:45.551
how you consume stuff that was
made right before the pandemic

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:49.656
For example, there's a priso
riot in the first episode.

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:51.991
-Right.
-And I was watching it thinking,

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:52.759 line:92%
"No one's wearing a mask.

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:56.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
Guys, you can't prison rio
without your masks."

00:04:56.362 --> 00:04:58.498 line:92%
-If you're going to riot,

00:04:58.498 --> 00:05:01.167 align:start size:94% position:6%
please throw chairs.
Don't punch.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:02.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
Throw chairs
from at least six feet.

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:05.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You can throw a chair
from six feet away.

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:06.306 line:92%
They're not heavy chairs.

00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:10.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
-If you can't, you can't riot.
It's that simple. Yeah.

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:16.249 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You -- You went to university
with Emma Thompson,

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:16.883 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
with Stephen Fry,

00:05:16.883 --> 00:05:21.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you had, at the time,
a very famous revue, I guess

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:24.457 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is that what you'd would cal
the Footlights?

00:05:24.457 --> 00:05:26.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, it is,
with a U-E at the end.

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:29.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Of course, yes.
-It's sort of old --

00:05:29.696 --> 00:05:31.564 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's almost sort of
vaudeville thing, really,

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:35.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
kind of sketches and songs
and monologues and --

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:38.237 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, I mean,
you did much the same.

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:42.942
You did exactly the same,
but more of it and better, Seth.

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:46.813 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I mean, certainly
I was doing stuff like tha

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:50.016
when I was in college, but you
guys were doing it in college,

00:05:50.016 --> 00:05:52.552 size:81% position:41%
and it became sort of
a bit of a phenomenon.

00:05:52.552 --> 00:05:55.455 align:start size:94% position:6%
That must've been --
Did you think at the time --

00:05:55.455 --> 00:05:59.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
Were you aware of how well
it was going for you?

00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:01.060 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-I was --

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.096 align:start size:94% position:6%
I knew it was pretty good.
I was proud of it.

00:06:03.096 --> 00:06:10.203
I've spent so much of my lif
in a position of abject shame,

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:11.104 line:92%
but this is one of those moments

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:13.039
where I actually felt quit
proud of what we were doing.

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:17.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think we all felt
we were onto something good.

00:06:17.777 --> 00:06:20.813 line:92%
But at that point, I was 21.

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:24.951 align:start size:94% position:6%
I had no idea
about doing this for a living.

00:06:24.951 --> 00:06:28.020 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, I mean,
it just didn't occur to anybod

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.591 align:start size:94% position:6%
to do such a thing.
It was just --

00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:33.226 line:92%
It was out of anybody's kent

00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:36.162 align:start size:94% position:6%
until a man turned up
with a big cigar

00:06:36.162 --> 00:06:38.531
and said "I'm an agent. Do you
want to do this for a living?"

00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:42.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I, you know --
"Well, I'll give it a go,"

00:06:42.402 --> 00:06:43.569 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and here I am.

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:47.874
-I think one of the first time
an American audience, obviously,

00:06:47.874 --> 00:06:50.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
got to know you
was from "House, M.D."

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:52.578 line:92%
Your father was a doctor,

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:55.782 align:start size:94% position:6%
and your son is
in medical school, correct

00:06:55.782 --> 00:06:56.949 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-I am proud to say

00:06:56.949 --> 00:07:04.190
that I am the ham in
the sandwich of genuine doctors.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:07.693 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yes, my father,
who I absolutely worshipped,

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:09.228 line:92%
was a general practitioner

00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:11.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
and my son is now
a junior doctor.

00:07:11.631 --> 00:07:14.000 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's just had
a pretty hairy four months

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.204 line:92%
dealing with COVID in the E.R.

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:20.807 align:start size:94% position:6%
But he's now just sort of
come out of that

00:07:20.807 --> 00:07:21.674 line:92%
and back on his rotation.

00:07:21.674 --> 00:07:25.378
And, at the moment, he's o
psychiatry and rather loving it.

00:07:25.378 --> 00:07:27.814 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm a bit nervous about it
I just don't know

00:07:27.814 --> 00:07:30.283 align:start size:94% position:6%
that one ought to have a child
who's a psychiatrist.

00:07:30.283 --> 00:07:32.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just find it
slightly unnerving.

00:07:32.585 --> 00:07:34.987 align:start size:94% position:6%
I feel there may be judgment
-Right, of course.

00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:37.557 align:start size:94% position:6%
It is interesting to me
that your son became a doctor.

00:07:37.557 --> 00:07:39.459 line:92%
You are obviously an actor

00:07:39.459 --> 00:07:42.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
You have a piano
behind you, I see.

00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:44.030 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Oh. Oh, that.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.232 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Your children
were musical, right?

00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:48.401 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughs ]

00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:51.003 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Uh, yes, they are.

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:52.772 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Yeah, absolutely.

00:07:52.772 --> 00:07:55.274 align:start size:94% position:6%
Oddly enough, I think there is
--

00:07:55.274 --> 00:07:58.744
It seems to me that doctor
are above-averagely medical.

00:07:58.744 --> 00:08:01.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
First of all, actually,
they're above-averagely funny.

00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:04.784 align:start size:94% position:6%
I find doctors funny.
They tend to be quite --

00:08:04.784 --> 00:08:09.121
You know, there's a hint of th
performer in a lot of doctors,

00:08:09.121 --> 00:08:12.258 align:start size:94% position:6%
but they also seem to be
very musical.

00:08:12.258 --> 00:08:14.260 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they are --
Yes, my kids are musical.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:17.630
We actually had a band
when they were teenagers.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:21.300 align:start size:94% position:6%
We would perform
as the Von Crap family.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:22.668 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ]

00:08:22.668 --> 00:08:25.538 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And, yes, we lived out
the title,

00:08:25.538 --> 00:08:28.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we did sort of wedding
and bar mitzvahs.

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:30.209 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ]

00:08:30.209 --> 00:08:32.211 line:92%
-Very, very unsuccessfully

00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:34.180 line:92%
but memorably, memorably for me.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:37.550 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I hope no one ever
recognizes your son,

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:38.184 size:88% position:44% line:92%
once he's a full doctor,

00:08:38.184 --> 00:08:41.020 align:start size:94% position:6%
as being one of the member
of the Von Crap family.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:43.923 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would have less confidence
going into surgery.

00:08:43.923 --> 00:08:47.426 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No. Yeah, "Wait a minute.
Get away from me."

00:08:47.426 --> 00:08:49.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, no,
that would be a problem.

00:08:49.362 --> 00:08:51.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
No, I think he could
live that down.

00:08:51.097 --> 00:08:52.565 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think he could
probably live that down.

00:08:52.565 --> 00:08:54.800 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I -- It's always so wonderful
to see you.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.869 size:81% position:41%
I do have to tell you,
I mentioned to my mom

00:08:56.869 --> 00:08:58.037 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you were going to be
on the show tonight,

00:08:58.037 --> 00:09:00.940 align:start size:94% position:6%
